<div class="mw-parser-output"><h1>Rapier</h1>
<p>Type of Weapon: Distanced Hand-to-hand.
</p><p>The name rapier is thought to have derived from the Spanish espada ropera, “sword of the robes.” The rapier was, essentially, a courtly gentleman’s blade rather than a soldier’s weapon. The rapier was a small-hilted, one-handed weapon with a relatively thin blade. Rapier combat developed to the use of an off-handed dagger and small shields like the buckler, targe, and lantern for parrying the opponent’s weapon. As the art of rapier defencing, or fencing for short, developed, the off-handed weapon and shields disappeared, leading into what we now think of as fencing. Rapier combat was the most involved, most studied form of sword fighting to date in history and it has been called an art form.
</p><p>This sword has a different feature from the others, as it has no edge. It was useless for slashing or hacking, but it was deadly when thrust. It was great for defense work and often had a hand basket or hand guard to protect the user. When the wielder knows how to use it, this is a very strong and deadly piece of equipment.
</p>
<h2><span class="mw-headline" id="In_Valhalla">In Valhalla</span></h2>
<p>In Valhalla the rapier is a weapon skill of its own, one of the swords (see <a cmd='#'>Help Weapons</a>): one-handed, it pierces, it leans on strength, with some dexterity, and it is of middling speed. It is taught by the barbarians from guild level 1, the fighters from guild level 1, the knights from guild level 1, the paladins from guild level 1, the rangers from guild level 1, the assassins from guild level 4 (up to 80%), the thieves from guild level 8 (up to 80%), the druids from guild level 12 (up to 50%) and the necromancers from guild level 25 (up to 70%).
</p><p><br />
